PROPOSAL 1 — state the equity boundary in §10
Section affected: §10 (What This Study Does Not Establish)

Primary driver: Alamoudi A, Gai Y, Zghebi SS, Kontopantelis E, Brown BC. "Patient safety and
health equity of AI-enabled triage tools in primary care: a systematic review." BMJ Health Care
Inform 2026;33(1). doi 10.1136/bmjhci-2026-102427, PMID 42951327. PROSPERO CRD420251179046.
University of Manchester. **Peer-reviewed; PubMed-verified 5 November 2026** — the record has been
in the library since 29 October on title and venue alone, because PubMed carried no abstract and
no author list for it then. Both are now indexed; this is the first cycle in which the paper could
actually be read.

What it found, searching PubMed, Embase, Web of Science and Scopus from January 2015 to November
2025 for prospective real-world evaluations of AI triage tools in primary care:

  1. Four studies met eligibility, and **all four evaluated rule-based or decision-tree tools.
     No machine-learning or generative-AI system met the eligibility criteria at all.**
  2. Safe triage 81–97.6%; under-triage 2–17.2%; over-triage 8–29%. The pooled safe-triage
     proportion across three studies (n = 1303) was 0.908 with a 95% CI of 0.319 to 0.995 and
     I² = 97.0% — which the authors state plainly is hypothesis-generating rather than a summary
     measure. We should quote their caution, not their point estimate.
  3. **No included study analysed differential triage accuracy by any sociodemographic factor,
     despite the demographic data being available.** The authors call this a systemic evidence gap
     and propose a Safety-Equity Evidence Mapping matrix to characterise it.
  4. Safety outcomes throughout "were confined to technical performance proxies rather than direct
     measures of harm."

Why this is a protocol gap rather than an enhancement. §10 opens by saying its boundaries are
"stated here rather than left for reviewers to find." Measured against that standard the section
is incomplete: the protocol contains **no** equity, sociodemographic, subgroup, health-literacy or
language-access term anywhere in its 33 pages — verified by grep this cycle, zero hits. That
silence was defensible while the question was merely foreseeable. It is now named, in a
peer-reviewed systematic review of this exact tool class, as the field's standing omission. An IRB
reviewing a patient-facing AI safety study will ask how the work bears on populations; the honest
answer is "it does not, and here is why," and that answer belongs in the section built to hold it.

What this proposal deliberately does NOT do. It adds **no** design element, no stratification, no
scenario authoring and no analysis. It makes no power claim and cannot disturb §8.4. The protocol's
dual register is a language-facility contrast held constant across models — it is not a proxy for
a population and must not be reported as one, which is itself part of what the boundary says.

